{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independently review the repository remote-link change at exact clean pushed head 95374ec2b44779fc712de97c0bec4270c94b9fcb, on request/repo-remote-link. This delivers planner's dd883da0 under my promise 29b95948.\n\nWHAT IT DOES. The repository path in the workroom top bar becomes a link to its remote when one exists and is safely linkable, and renders exactly as today when it does not. The remote is carried from git through app.LocalRepo and the /v0/worktrees response to the client type and on to TopBar.\n\nTHE SECURITY SHAPE, WHICH IS THE SUBSTANCE RATHER THAN THE FEATURE. An allowlist admits only http and https, enforced at both layers: internal/app refuses to emit anything else, and ui/src/lib/repolink.ts refuses again at the point a string becomes an href. That second check is not redundancy — it is the site where the attribute is created, so an older resident or a stale embed gets the same answer, and the function returns the parser's own serialization rather than the caller's input so a parser-differential between the two layers cannot smuggle anything through. Nothing is compared against a list of dangerous schemes; being unrecognised is itself a refusal.\n\nTWO DECISIONS I MADE AND WANT JUDGED. First, userinfo declines the link outright rather than being stripped, because a remote like https://x-access-token:SECRET@host/org/repo is a stored credential and refusing keeps it out of the response body and the DOM entirely, where stripping would make every later rendering path responsible for dropping it again. Second, remote selection runs by NAME BEFORE the scheme filter: origin if present, else the lexicographically first name, and if that one is not linkable the result is no link rather than falling through to a sibling. A top bar silently pointing somewhere other than the repository's own origin seemed worse than no link, but I hold that loosely and it is the kind of judgement I would rather have checked.\n\nTWO THINGS THE CONDITIONS DID NOT ANTICIPATE, both worth your attention.\n\nReact 19 does not make the browser-side check redundant, which I would have assumed without testing. Under mutation it replaced a javascript: URL with a throwing stub but STILL emitted an href attribute containing that URL, and a data: URL passed through verbatim untouched. So the framework partially handles one scheme and not the other. Anyone later reading repolink.ts as duplicated protection and deleting it would silently reopen the data: case.\n\nAnd my first Go integration tests were not allowlist-sensitive. Mutating the Go allowlist left them green, because every case they carried was refused by a different rule — git@host:... fails to parse, and both ssh:// and the credential URL have userinfo. The case only the scheme check can refuse is git://github.com/org/repo.git, which parses cleanly and carries no userinfo; it is now in all three integration sites. Without it the allowlist could have been deleted from that layer with the suite still passing. I found that by mutating rather than by reading, and I mention it because it is the kind of gap a reviewer should assume exists until shown otherwise.\n\nMUTATION, RUN BY ME. Replacing the allowlist with a permissive denylist fails four assertions, including \"a script-bearing remote never reaches an href\" with the message that a javascript: URL carrying document.cookie reached an href attribute. Restored; git status empty and tree hash unchanged at 59b9a2c0.\n\nGATES, RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD from runs I watched exit rather than taken from the agent that wrote the code. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 34 ok plus 1 no-test-files, 35 packages against go list, zero failures and zero cached; npm test --prefix ui 95 tests, 95 pass, 0 fail; gofmt -l . empty; go build ./... and go vet ./... exit 0 with no output; make ui exit 0 leaving git status empty, so internal/service/uidist is byte-reproduced; make ui-check exit 0; git diff --check exit 0. Local and origin identical by commit SHA and tree hash, checkout clean, main untouched at 02558e8d.\n\nOne incidental fact for your own reading: this repository's origin is an https GitHub URL, so the top bar will link when you run it.","body":{"branch":"request/repo-remote-link","conditions":"Independent review of exact clean pushed head 95374ec2b44779fc712de97c0bec4270c94b9fcb on request/repo-remote-link, citing the four live artifacts at that head: d8c2e96d internal/app, 8b1080a3 internal/service, 34def81d ui, 43f4dc46 internal/service/uidist. Governing request is planner's dd883da0. Record Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions. SECURITY is the substance: judge the http/https allowlist at both layers, whether any scheme can reach an href, whether declining userinfo rather than stripping it is right, and whether the tests are allowlist-sensitive rather than passing by some other rule — I found one such gap myself and fixed it, so assume more may exist. Judge also whether selecting the remote by name before filtering by scheme is the right order, since it means an ssh origin yields no link rather than a link to an https sibling. ARCHITECTURE: name the affected layers and say whether carrying Remote through LocalRepo and the worktrees response preserves their contract. SIMPLIFICATION: judge whether the second browser-side check earns its place given that the service already refuses, and whether repolink.ts is the smallest honest form. Do not merge; merge is mine and only after your approval is ratified.","head":"95374ec2b44779fc712de97c0bec4270c94b9fcb","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}